{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T5RssP9ETPD2MgRZiFgDCo","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on the context filesystem, I found the answer:\\\\n\\\\n**The workaround:** When older-version metadata requests lack a rack ID, the design encodes the rack information in the client ID. With this approach, the system \\\\\\\"lies\\\\\\\" to the consumer by replacing the leader with one of the replicas from the same rack if available.\\\\n\\\\nThis is explained in the discussion about KIP-1150 (Diskless Topics) metadata handling:\\\\n\\\\n> \\\\\\\"However, as an workaround, we can encode the rack information in the client ID. In this case we \\\\\\\"lie\\\\\\\" to the consumer by replacing the leader with one of the replicas from the same rack if available.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n**File:** `/_intake/2026/01/23/mail-dd72e188e244a14eeb227fa2-9b009d05.md` (lines 27-28)\\\\n\\\\nThe context explains that for newer MetadataRequest versions containing a RackId field, the system returns truthful information about leaders and replicas. However, for older clients without rack information in their requests, this workaround allows encoding the rack information in the client ID to still provide rack-aligned responses by returning a same-rack replica instead of the actual leader when beneficial.\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"store\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/12/github-api-apache-kafka-review-comment-PRRC_kwDOACG9q86f1Xlr-f172a636.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/14/mail-916e7f6fbe371d344b9ff725-3f56d8ed.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/23/mail-dd72e188e244a14eeb227fa2-9b009d05.md\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":32.68,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":2,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":202667,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":43084,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.11763569999999998,\\\"input_tokens\\\":106,\\\"output_tokens\\\":2219}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8074dead-0aa1-4180-821d-3faa8dc5ca86","uuid":"bfcf1dc0-1a90-4898-ae38-c99706e98273","timestamp":"2026-09-27T18:48:40.068Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on the context filesystem, I found the answer:\\\\n\\\\n**The workaround:** When older-version metadata requests lack a rack ID, the design encodes the rack information in the client ID. With this approach, the system \\\\\\\"lies\\\\\\\" to the consumer by replacing the leader with one of the replicas from the same rack if available.\\\\n\\\\nThis is explained in the discussion about KIP-1150 (Diskless Topics) metadata handling:\\\\n\\\\n> \\\\\\\"However, as an workaround, we can encode the rack information in the client ID. In this case we \\\\\\\"lie\\\\\\\" to the consumer by replacing the leader with one of the replicas from the same rack if available.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n**File:** `/_intake/2026/01/23/mail-dd72e188e244a14eeb227fa2-9b009d05.md` (lines 27-28)\\\\n\\\\nThe context explains that for newer MetadataRequest versions containing a RackId field, the system returns truthful information about leaders and replicas. However, for older clients without rack information in their requests, this workaround allows encoding the rack information in the client ID to still provide rack-aligned responses by returning a same-rack replica instead of the actual leader when beneficial.\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"store\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/12/github-api-apache-kafka-review-comment-PRRC_kwDOACG9q86f1Xlr-f172a636.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/14/mail-916e7f6fbe371d344b9ff725-3f56d8ed.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/23/mail-dd72e188e244a14eeb227fa2-9b009d05.md\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":32.68,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":2,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":202667,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":43084,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.11763569999999998,\\\"input_tokens\\\":106,\\\"output_tokens\\\":2219}}\"}","_meta":{"fastmcp":{"wrap_result":true}},"structuredContent":{"result":"{\"answer\":\"Based on the context filesystem, I found the answer:\\n\\n**The workaround:** When older-version metadata requests lack a rack ID, the design encodes the rack information in the client ID. With this approach, the system \\\"lies\\\" to the consumer by replacing the leader with one of the replicas from the same rack if available.\\n\\nThis is explained in the discussion about KIP-1150 (Diskless Topics) metadata handling:\\n\\n> \\\"However, as an workaround, we can encode the rack information in the client ID. In this case we \\\"lie\\\" to the consumer by replacing the leader with one of the replicas from the same rack if available.\\\"\\n\\n**File:** `/_intake/2026/01/23/mail-dd72e188e244a14eeb227fa2-9b009d05.md` (lines 27-28)\\n\\nThe context explains that for newer MetadataRequest versions containing a RackId field, the system returns truthful information about leaders and replicas. However, for older clients without rack information in their requests, this workaround allows encoding the rack information in the client ID to still provide rack-aligned responses by returning a same-rack replica instead of the actual leader when beneficial.\",\"error\":null,\"files\":[\"store\",\"_intake/2026/01/12/github-api-apache-kafka-review-comment-PRRC_kwDOACG9q86f1Xlr-f172a636.md\",\"_intake/2026/01/14/mail-916e7f6fbe371d344b9ff725-3f56d8ed.md\",\"_intake/2026/01/23/mail-dd72e188e244a14eeb227fa2-9b009d05.md\"],\"seconds\":32.68,\"truncated\":false,\"turns\":2,\"usage\":{\"cache_read_tokens\":202667,\"cache_write_tokens\":43084,\"cost_usd\":0.11763569999999998,\"input_tokens\":106,\"output_tokens\":2219}}"}}}
